"[2511.09179v1] A Hybrid Search for Complex Table Question Answering in Securities Report (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09179v1)\nciteturn5951view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 10 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09179v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 201\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:  cite57†Image: Cornell University L3: \nL4:  We gratefully acknowledge support from the Simons Foundation, cite1†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , and all contributors. cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org L5: \nL6: cite58†Image: arxiv logo > cite3†cs > arXiv:2511.09179v1\nL7: \nL8: cite4†Help†info.arxiv.org | cite5†Advanced Search L9: \nL10:  All fields Title Author Abstract Comments Journal reference ACM classification MSC classification Report number arXiv identifier DOI ORCID arXiv author ID Help pages Full text\nL11: \nL12: Search\nL13: cite59†Image: arXiv logo L14: \nL15: cite6† †www.cornell.edu L16: \nL17:  open search\nL18: \nL19: GO\nL20: \nL21: open navigation menu\nL22: ## quick links\nL23: \nL24:   * cite7†Login L25:   * cite4†Help Pages†info.arxiv.org L26:   * cite8†About†info.arxiv.org L27: \nL28: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL29: \nL30: arXiv:2511.09179v1 (cs)\nL31: \nL32: [Submitted on 12 Nov 2025]\nL33: # Title:A Hybrid Search for Complex Table Question Answering in Securities Report\nL34: \nL35: Authors:cite9†Daiki Shirafuji , cite10†Koji Tanaka , cite11†Tatsuhiko Saito L36: \nL37: View a PDF of the paper titled A Hybrid Search for Complex Table Question Answering in Securities Report, by Daiki Shirafuji and 2 other authors\nL38: \nL39: cite12†View PDF cite13†HTML (experimental) L40: > Abstract:Recently, Large Language Models (LLMs) are gaining increased attention in the domain of Table Question Answering (TQA), particularly for extracting information from tables in documents. However, directly entering entire tables as long text into LLMs often leads to incorrect answers because most LLMs cannot inherently capture complex table structures. In this paper, we propose a cell extraction method for TQA without manual identification, even for complex table headers.\nL41: Our approach estimates table headers by computing similarities between a given question and individual cells via a hybrid retrieval mechanism that integrates a language model and TF-IDF. We then select as the answer the cells at the intersection of the most relevant row and column. Furthermore, the language model is trained using contrastive learning on a small dataset of question-header pairs to enhance performance. We evaluated our approach in the TQA dataset from the U4 shared task at NTCIR-18.\nL42: The experimental results show that our pipeline achieves an accuracy of 74.6\\%, outperforming existing LLMs such as GPT-4o mini~(63.9\\%). In the future, although we used traditional encoder models for retrieval in this study, we plan to incorporate more efficient text-search models to improve performance and narrow the gap with human evaluation results.\nL43: Comments: Accepted to IIAI AAI 2025\nL44: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL45: Cite as: cite14†arXiv:2511.09179 [cs.CL]\nL46:   (or  cite15†arXiv:2511.09179v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL47:   cite16†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.09179†doi.org L48: \nL49: Focus to learn more\nL50: \nL51:   arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL52: ## Submission history\nL53: \nL54: From: Daiki Shirafuji [cite17†view email ]\nL55: [v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 10:19:27 UTC (773 KB)\nL56: \nL57: Full-text links:\nL58: ## Access Paper:\nL59: \nL60:  View a PDF of the paper titled A Hybrid Search for Complex Table Question Answering in Securities Report, by Daiki Shirafuji and 2 other authors\nL61: \nL62:   * cite12†View PDF L63:   * cite13†HTML (experimental) L64:   * cite18†TeX Source  L65: \nL66: cite19† view license †creativecommons.org