nL199:   * cite55†Web Accessibility Assistance†info.arxiv.org L200:   * cite56†arXiv Operational Status †status.arxiv.org --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2511.09185v1] Context is Enough: Empirical Validation of $\\textit{Sequentiality}$ on Essays (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09185v1)\nciteturn5951view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 7 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09185v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 201\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:  cite56†Image: Cornell University L3: \nL4:  We gratefully acknowledge support from the Simons Foundation, cite1†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , and all contributors. cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org L5: \nL6: cite57†Image: arxiv logo > cite3†cs > arXiv:2511.09185v1\nL7: \nL8: cite4†Help†info.arxiv.org | cite5†Advanced Search L9: \nL10:  All fields Title Author Abstract Comments Journal reference ACM classification MSC classification Report number arXiv identifier DOI ORCID arXiv author ID Help pages Full text\nL11: \nL12: Search\nL13: cite58†Image: arXiv logo L14: \nL15: cite6† †www.cornell.edu L16: \nL17:  open search\nL18: \nL19: GO\nL20: \nL21: open navigation menu\nL22: ## quick links\nL23: \nL24:   * cite7†Login L25:   * cite4†Help Pages†info.arxiv.org L26:   * cite8†About†info.arxiv.org L27: \nL28: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL29: \nL30: arXiv:2511.09185v1 (cs)\nL31: \nL32: [Submitted on 12 Nov 2025]\nL33: # Title:Context is Enough: Empirical Validation of $\\textit{Sequentiality}$ on Essays\nL34: \nL35: Authors:cite9†Amal Sunny , cite10†Advay Gupta , cite11†Vishnu Sreekumar L36: \nL37: View a PDF of the paper titled Context is Enough: Empirical Validation of $\\textit{Sequentiality}$ on Essays, by Amal Sunny and 2 other authors\nL38: \nL39: cite12†View PDF cite13†HTML (experimental) L40: > Abstract:Recent work has proposed using Large Language Models (LLMs) to quantify narrative flow through a measure called sequentiality, which combines topic and contextual terms. A recent critique argued that the original results were confounded by how topics were selected for the topic-based component, and noted that the metric had not been validated against ground-truth measures of flow. That work proposed using only the contextual term as a more conceptually valid and interpretable alternative.\nL41: In this paper, we empirically validate that proposal. Using two essay datasets with human-annotated trait scores, ASAP++ and ELLIPSE, we show that the contextual version of sequentiality aligns more closely with human assessments of discourse-level traits such as Organization and Cohesion.\nL42: While zero-shot prompted LLMs predict trait scores more accurately than the contextual measure alone, the contextual measure adds more predictive value than both the topic-only and original sequentiality formulations when combined with standard linguistic features. Notably, this combination also outperforms the zero-shot LLM predictions, highlighting the value of explicitly modeling sentence-to-sentence flow.\nL43: Our findings support the use of context-based sequentiality as a validated, interpretable, and complementary feature for automated essay scoring and related NLP tasks.\nL44: Comments: 9 pages, 2 figures\nL45: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL46: Cite as: cite14†arXiv:2511.09185 [cs.CL]\nL47:   (or  cite15†arXiv:2511.09185v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL48:   cite16†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.09185†doi.org L49: \nL50: Focus to learn more\nL51: \nL52:   arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL53: ## Submission history\nL54: \nL55: From: Amal Sunny [cite17†view email ]\nL56: [v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 10:31:07 UTC (204 KB)\nL57: \nL58: Full-text links:\nL59: ## Access Paper:\nL60: \nL61:  View a PDF of the paper titled Context is Enough: Empirical Validation of $\\textit{Sequentiality}$ o