ibe to arXiv mailings Click here to subscribe cite51† Subscribe†info.arxiv.org L196:   * cite52†Copyright†info.arxiv.org L197:   * cite53†Privacy Policy†info.arxiv.org L198: \nL199:   * cite54†Web Accessibility Assistance†info.arxiv.org L200:   * cite55†arXiv Operational Status †status.arxiv.org --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2511.09197v1] The Learning Dynamics of Subword Segmentation for Morphologically Diverse Languages (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09197v1)\nciteturn5951view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09197v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 161\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 12 Nov 2025 (this version), latest version 19 Nov 2025 (cite5†v2 )]\nL9: # Title:The Learning Dynamics of Subword Segmentation for Morphologically Diverse Languages\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Francois Meyer , cite7†Jan Buys L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled The Learning Dynamics of Subword Segmentation for Morphologically Diverse Languages, by Francois Meyer and Jan Buys\nL14: \nL15: cite8†View PDF cite9†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Subword segmentation is typically applied in preprocessing and stays fixed during training. Alternatively, it can be learned during training to optimise the training objective.\nL17: In this paper we study the learning dynamics of subword segmentation: if a language model can dynamically optimise tokenisation, how do its subwords evolve during pretraining and finetuning? To explore this, we extend the subword segmental language model (SSLM), a framework for learning subwords during training, to support pretraining and finetuning.\nL18: We train models for three typologically diverse languages to study learning dynamics across the morphological spectrum: Isi-Xhosa is conjunctive (long word forms composed of many morphemes), Setswana is disjunctive (morphemes written as separate words), and English represents a typological middle ground. We analyse subword dynamics from a linguistic perspective, tracking morphology, productivity, and fertility.\nL19: We identify four stages of subword learning, with the morphologically complex isi-Xhosa exhibiting greater instability. During finetuning, subword boundaries shift to become finer-grained. Lastly, we show that learnable subwords offers a promising approach to improve text generation and cross-lingual transfer for low-resource, morphologically complex languages.\nL20: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite10†arXiv:2511.09197 [cs.CL]\nL22:    | (or cite11†arXiv:2511.09197v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL23:    | cite12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.09197†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Francois Meyer [cite13†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 10:52:33 UTC (10,172 KB)\nL28: cite5†[v2] Wed, 19 Nov 2025 09:30:00 UTC (1,454 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled The Learning Dynamics of Subword Segmentation for Morphologically Diverse Languages, by Francois Meyer and Jan Buys\nL35: \nL36:   * cite8†View PDF L37:   * cite9†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite14†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite15†view license†creativecommons.org L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.CL\nL44: \nL45: cite16†< prev |   cite17†next > L46: \nL47: cite18†new | cite19†recent | cite20†2025-11 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite21†cs L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite22†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.ha